@* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Site/Account/SignOut.aspx (+ .aspx.cs) -> Components/Pages/Site/Account/SignOut.razor
   (checklist task 96). The route is the one the migrated layouts link to (Specialist 5).

   The legacy Page_Init was:
       this.IsLandingPage = true;
       this.Session.Remove("UserName");
       FormsAuthentication.SignOut();
       this.Response.Redirect("~/SignIn.aspx");

   WHY THIS PAGE FORCE-LOADS AN ENDPOINT. Removing a session entry and signing a cookie out are
   both response mutations, and HttpContext is READ-ONLY inside a circuit -- doing them from
   component code silently does nothing. Specialist 4 therefore owns the sign-out as the HTTP
   endpoint AuthEndpoints.SignOutRoute ("/_auth/signout"), which performs exactly the three steps
   above: SessionHelper.RemoveSessionKey("UserName") (VERIFIED present), then
   MixERPAuthentication.SignOutAsync(context), then Response.Redirect(MixERPAuthentication.LoginPath)
   -- the migrated form of "~/SignIn.aspx". This component's single job is to force-load that url,
   which it does on its first render (an interactive navigation would stay inside the circuit and
   never reach the endpoint).

   This page deliberately does NOT declare @page "/_auth/signout": the endpoint owns that url, and
   a component claiming it would shadow the only code that can actually sign the user out.

   The body of the legacy page was EMPTY (all four <asp:Content> blocks were blank) because it
   redirected before rendering. The same holds here apart from the one-line notice below, which is
   what the user sees for the moment between the first render and the browser following the
   force-load.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
@page "/Site/Account/SignOut"
@layout BackendLayout
@inherits MixERPAuthorizedPageBase

<p>@Titles.SignOut</p>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    ///     Page_Init: <c>this.IsLandingPage = true</c> (no menu entry -- the policy check must not
    ///     run against the sign-out page). Set in the constructor because the base activation
    ///     sequence that reads it runs in OnInitializedAsync.
    /// </summary>
    public SignOut()
    {
        this.IsLandingPage = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     Session.Remove("UserName") + FormsAuthentication.SignOut() + Response.Redirect: all
    ///     three happen on the /_auth/signout endpoint, which is reached with forceLoad so the
    ///     browser performs a real HTTP request and the endpoint's own redirect to the sign-in page
    ///     takes effect.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        await base.OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender).ConfigureAwait(true);

        if (firstRender)
        {
            this.NavigationManager.NavigateTo(AuthEndpoints.SignOutRoute, true);
        }
    }
}
